### What are `anyMatch`, `allMatch`, and `noneMatch`?

All three are terminal operations defined on `Stream`. Each takes a `Predicate` (a function that returns a boolean) and itself returns a primitive `boolean`. They are the standard way to answer validation and existence questions over a collection without writing an explicit loop with a flag.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class MatchDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> batch = List.of(
                new Employee(1, "Rajesh", 34, "Trading", new BigDecimal("1200000")),
                new Employee(2, "Priya", 29, "Risk", new BigDecimal("850000")),
                new Employee(3, "Pankaj", 41, "Settlement", new BigDecimal("990000"))
        );
        Set<String> blocked = Set.of("Sanctions", "Frozen");

        boolean hasHighEarner = batch.stream().anyMatch(e -> e.salary().compareTo(new BigDecimal("1000000")) > 0);
        boolean allAdults = batch.stream().allMatch(e -> e.age() >= 18);
        boolean noneBlocked = batch.stream().noneMatch(e -> blocked.contains(e.department()));

        System.out.println("hasHighEarner = " + hasHighEarner);
        System.out.println("allAdults     = " + allAdults);
        System.out.println("noneBlocked   = " + noneBlocked);
    }
}
```

```text
hasHighEarner = true
allAdults     = true
noneBlocked   = true
```

The `Employee` record used throughout:

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public record Employee(long id, String name, int age, String department, BigDecimal salary) { }
```

### Why do these exist instead of `filter().count()`?

You could filter and count, but that always scans the whole stream and allocates intermediate work. The matching terminals express intent directly and short-circuit. Asking "is there any high earner?" should stop at the first high earner, not count all of them.

```java
// Works but wasteful: counts every match even though one is enough
boolean hasHighEarner = batch.stream()
        .filter(e -> e.salary().compareTo(new BigDecimal("1000000")) > 0)
        .count() > 0;

// Better: stops at the first match
boolean hasHighEarnerFast = batch.stream()
        .anyMatch(e -> e.salary().compareTo(new BigDecimal("1000000")) > 0);
```

### How does short-circuiting work?

Short-circuiting means the operation stops traversing the stream as soon as the result is decided. `anyMatch` returns `true` at the first match. `allMatch` returns `false` at the first failure. `noneMatch` returns `false` at the first match. On a large stream, the operation often reads only a few elements.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class ShortCircuitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean found = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 1_000_000)
                .peek(n -> { if (n <= 5) System.out.println("checking " + n); })
                .anyMatch(n -> n == 3);
        System.out.println("found = " + found);
    }
}
```

```text
checking 1
checking 2
checking 3
found = true
```

The stream stops at 3 and never checks 4 through one million.

### What do they return for an empty stream?

This is the detail interviewers probe. The answers follow formal logic:

- `anyMatch` on an empty stream is `false`, because there is nothing that could match.
- `allMatch` on an empty stream is `true`, because "every element satisfies the predicate" is vacuously true when there are no elements.
- `noneMatch` on an empty stream is `true`, because "no element matches" is vacuously true when there are no elements.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

import java.util.List;

public class EmptyStreamDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> empty = List.of();
        System.out.println("anyMatch  = " + empty.stream().anyMatch(n -> n > 0));
        System.out.println("allMatch  = " + empty.stream().allMatch(n -> n > 0));
        System.out.println("noneMatch = " + empty.stream().noneMatch(n -> n > 0));
    }
}
```

```text
anyMatch  = false
allMatch  = true
noneMatch = true
```

### How are the three related to each other?

| Operation | True when | Stops at | Empty stream | Logical form |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `anyMatch(p)` | at least one matches | first match | `false` | exists p |
| `allMatch(p)` | every element matches | first failure | `true` | for all p |
| `noneMatch(p)` | no element matches | first match | `true` | for all not-p |

There are logical identities between them. `noneMatch(p)` equals `allMatch(not p)`, and `anyMatch(p)` equals `!noneMatch(p)`.

```java
boolean a = batch.stream().noneMatch(e -> e.age() < 18);
boolean b = batch.stream().allMatch(e -> !(e.age() < 18));
// a and b are always equal
System.out.println(a == b);
```

```text
true
```

### What are common mistakes?

The first mistake is assuming `allMatch` is `false` on an empty stream. Developers write a validation gate and are surprised when an empty batch passes every "all" check.

```java
// WRONG assumption: expecting this to reject an empty batch
boolean everyoneApproved = batch.stream().allMatch(Employee::approved);
if (everyoneApproved) {
    process(batch); // an EMPTY batch slips through, because allMatch is true
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: guard emptiness explicitly when it matters
if (!batch.isEmpty() && batch.stream().allMatch(Employee::approved)) {
    process(batch);
}
```

The second mistake is calling a match twice on the same stream object, which throws because streams are single-use.

```java
// WRONG: stream already consumed
Stream<Employee> s = batch.stream();
boolean a = s.anyMatch(e -> e.age() > 60);
boolean b = s.allMatch(e -> e.age() >= 18); // IllegalStateException: stream has already been operated upon
```

```java
// CORRECT: start a fresh stream for each check
boolean a = batch.stream().anyMatch(e -> e.age() > 60);
boolean b = batch.stream().allMatch(e -> e.age() >= 18);
```

### What about parallel streams and side effects?

The three terminals are safe to run in parallel because they only read and combine booleans. The predicate must not have side effects, because in parallel it runs on many elements concurrently and short-circuiting means some elements are never evaluated at all.

```java
// WRONG: mutating shared state inside the predicate
List<String> log = new ArrayList<>();
batch.parallelStream().anyMatch(e -> { log.add(e.name()); return e.age() > 60; });
// log is unpredictable: order, count, and thread safety are all broken
```

```java
// CORRECT: a pure predicate with no side effects
boolean hasSenior = batch.parallelStream().anyMatch(e -> e.age() > 60);
```

### When should you use each one?

Use `anyMatch` to confirm existence, for example whether any candidate in a batch is a high earner who needs extra approval. Use `allMatch` to confirm a universal rule, for example that every candidate is an adult. Use `noneMatch` to confirm a forbidden condition is absent, for example that no candidate comes from a sanctioned department. Each returns a clean boolean that feeds straight into a compliance gate.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

import com.bank.model.Employee;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class OnboardingGate {
    public boolean canOnboard(List<Employee> batch, Set<String> blocked) {
        if (batch.isEmpty()) {
            return false; // guard the vacuous-true trap
        }
        boolean allAdults = batch.stream().allMatch(e -> e.age() >= 18);
        boolean noneBlocked = batch.stream().noneMatch(e -> blocked.contains(e.department()));
        return allAdults && noneBlocked;
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often ask: what does `allMatch` return on an empty stream, and why? The answer is `true`, vacuously, because there is no element that violates the predicate. They may ask how `noneMatch(p)` relates to `allMatch`, where the answer is `noneMatch(p)` equals `allMatch(not p)`. They may ask whether these scan the whole stream, where the answer is no, they short-circuit. Finally they may ask why the predicate must be side-effect free, where the answer is short-circuiting and parallelism mean the predicate runs an unpredictable number of times on unpredictable threads.
